schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "What Is a Local Node?"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    A practical guide for people ready to build, defining a "local node" as a
    place-based community group using Web3 tools and regenerative principles
    to coordinate real-world action in one specific area — simultaneously
    self-governing and network-connected. Covers what nodes do (pool
    resources, fund public goods, onboard newcomers, run a steady meetup
    cadence, connect outward), when Web3 tooling actually earns its place vs.
    a spreadsheet, the Localism Fund / quadratic-funding network-funding
    picture, common structural patterns, a three-stage Seed/Sapling/Canopy
    maturity model (ReFi DAO), and the single biggest failure mode: bus-factor-
    of-one burnout.
  audience: people ready to build a place-based Web3-enabled regen community group
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Node autonomy vs. central overreach — the article frames "subsidiarity"
      (decisions at the most local level able to make them) as the design
      answer to the fear that a network could swallow a local project.
    - >-
      Technology-first vs. relationships-first — "a node with strong
      relationships and a Google Sheet beats a node with flawless tokenomics
      and no trust."
  related_concepts:
    - subsidiarity
    - local-node
  related_resources:
    - greenpill-network
    - safe-multisig
    - snapshot
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  high_risk: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-local-node.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
